1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,914
Lesson 10.1.

2
00:00:02,294 --> 00:00:03,865
Transformer review.

3
00:00:04,983 --> 00:00:09,304
Before taking a language model to
thermodynamic hardware, we must know

4
00:00:09,304 --> 00:00:13,624
precisely what a transformer does
and where it spends time and energy.

5
00:00:14,004 --> 00:00:18,061
This lesson is a targeted review:
the parts of the block, how many

6
00:00:18,061 --> 00:00:22,486
operations each token costs, the cache
memory and the crucial difference

7
00:00:22,486 --> 00:00:26,174
between processing the prompt
and generating token by token.

8
00:00:27,283 --> 00:00:29,539
A transformer is a stack of identical

9
00:00:29,539 --> 00:00:32,160
blocks on a residual
stream of dimension d.

10
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:36,119
Each block has a normalization,
an attention that mixes

11
00:00:36,119 --> 00:00:39,828
information across positions,
another normalization and a

12
00:00:39,828 --> 00:00:44,253
two-layer network, the MLP, that
processes each position separately.

13
00:00:44,633 --> 00:00:48,381
Attention computes queries,
keys and values, and weights the

14
00:00:48,381 --> 00:00:52,315
values with a softmax of the
products between queries and keys.

15
00:00:53,417 --> 00:00:57,437
Each block has about 12 d²
parameters: four in the attention

16
00:00:57,437 --> 00:01:01,457
projections and eight in the
MLP with a four-fold expansion.

17
00:01:01,837 --> 00:01:05,249
Processing a token costs about
two operations per parameter.

18
00:01:05,629 --> 00:01:10,794
Attention adds a term that grows
with the context L: 4 L d per block.

19
00:01:11,174 --> 00:01:14,601
For d = 4096 and a context
of four thousand, that is

20
00:01:14,601 --> 00:01:18,094
sixty-seven million attention
operations against four

21
00:01:18,094 --> 00:01:21,785
hundred million for the
matrices: the matrices dominate.

22
00:01:22,917 --> 00:01:26,644
When generating, the keys and
values of previous tokens do

23
00:01:26,644 --> 00:01:30,050
not change, so they are
stored: that is the KV cache.

24
00:01:30,430 --> 00:01:32,916
Its size per token is
two times the number of

25
00:01:32,916 --> 00:01:35,236
layers times d times the bytes per number.

26
00:01:35,616 --> 00:01:39,419
In a seven-billion-parameter
model with thirty-two layers and

27
00:01:39,419 --> 00:01:43,595
sixteen-bit precision, that is half
a megabyte per token: with four

28
00:01:43,595 --> 00:01:47,648
thousand tokens of context, more
than two gigabytes per sequence.

29
00:01:48,750 --> 00:01:52,022
Processing the prompt,
the prefill, performs many

30
00:01:52,022 --> 00:01:55,829
operations per byte of weights
read: it is compute-bound.

31
00:01:56,209 --> 00:01:59,226
Generating token by token,
the decode, reads all the

32
00:01:59,226 --> 00:02:01,952
weights to produce a
single token per sequence.

33
00:02:02,332 --> 00:02:06,132
With the roofline model, a modern
GPU needs about two hundred

34
00:02:06,132 --> 00:02:09,745
ninety-five operations per
byte to run at full throughput.

35
00:02:10,125 --> 00:02:15,762
With batch one, decode does one: its
maximum utilization is 0.3 percent.

36
00:02:16,142 --> 00:02:21,279
With batch sixteen, five percent;
with sixty-four, twenty-two.

37
00:02:22,383 --> 00:02:23,946
Here the opportunity appears.

38
00:02:24,326 --> 00:02:27,939
In a spatial TSU, the weights
live on the chip itself as

39
00:02:27,939 --> 00:02:32,068
couplings, so they need not be
read from memory for every token.

40
00:02:32,448 --> 00:02:35,707
Batch-one decode, the
worst case for a GPU,

41
00:02:35,707 --> 00:02:38,587
does not penalize a TSU's utilization.

42
00:02:38,967 --> 00:02:43,898
In contrast, prefill, which the GPU does
very well, offers no clear advantage.

43
00:02:44,278 --> 00:02:49,602
That is why thermodynamic language models
aim mainly at interactive generation.

44
00:02:50,717 --> 00:02:54,675
With these counts one estimates
operations per token, sizes the

45
00:02:54,675 --> 00:02:58,131
cache and predicts utilization
with the roofline model.

46
00:02:58,511 --> 00:03:02,381
In lab twenty-eight you will measure
prefill and decode on your GPU.

47
00:03:03,483 --> 00:03:08,380
Common mistakes: using prefill
utilization to estimate decode energy.

48
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:11,154
Ignoring the cache in the memory budget.

49
00:03:11,534 --> 00:03:14,738
Assuming attention dominates
at moderate context.

50
00:03:15,118 --> 00:03:19,113
And claiming a TSU advantage
for prefill-dominated workloads.

51
00:03:20,217 --> 00:03:24,018
In summary: a block has about
12 d² parameters and each

52
00:03:24,018 --> 00:03:27,888
token costs two operations
per parameter plus attention.

53
00:03:28,268 --> 00:03:33,037
The KV cache takes half a megabyte
per token in a seven-billion model.

54
00:03:33,417 --> 00:03:39,116
Prefill is compute-bound and decode
memory-bound, with very low utilization at small

55
00:03:39,116 --> 00:03:44,137
batch: exactly where a TSU, with the
weights on chip, has its opportunity.

56
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:48,247
To practice: count the
parameters of a block.

57
00:03:48,627 --> 00:03:50,313
Compute the cache per token.

58
00:03:50,693 --> 00:03:54,408
Compute the maximum decode
utilization with batch thirty-two.

59
00:03:54,788 --> 00:03:58,152
And explain why prefill is compute-bound.
